From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] dcomp: Objects that exist.

DirectComposition is how a modern Windows program gets its swapchain onto
the screen without owning a window: it makes a device, a target for its
HWND, and a visual whose content is the swapchain, then roots the visual
in the target and commits. GTA IV reaches this port through 1911.dll and
does exactly that, and the stub answered every call with E_NOTIMPL and
nothing written where a pointer was meant to go.

That cost two separate failures, a build apart.

First the device. The loader does not check the HRESULT, and read a field
at +0x2c off the NULL it was left with:

    fixme:dcomp:DCompositionCreateDevice 00000000, {c37ea93a-...}, 002541D0.
    err:wow:raise_guest_exception guest exception 0xc0000005 at eip 0x79f9d308
                                  esp 0x21fe64 address 0x2c

That crash had been read as a DRM check for most of a day.

Then, with a device and with the composition swapchain created and filled
in -- images, a command pool, four command buffers, five semaphores, a
fence -- the log stops on one line and the game idles at 0% with the
screen black, the display already handed over to Vulkan:

    fixme:dcomp:device_CreateTargetForHwnd iface 00BC5570: stub.

So: a device, a target that accepts a root, and a visual that accepts
content and succeeds at every placement call. Commit returns S_OK. There
is no compositor on this console and no need for one -- the swapchain
presents itself to the scanout through RADV's WSI -- so what this
implements is the shape of the thing, not the thing. Everything with no
bearing on getting a swapchain shown still answers E_NOTIMPL: surfaces,
transforms, effects, clips, animations. A caller is better told no than
handed an object that silently does nothing.

⚠ The methods carry their real signatures and the vtables are the
generated typed ones. An earlier draft declared every method WINAPI taking
only iface and cast them into a void * vtable; stdcall is callee-pops, so
CreateTargetForHwnd popped four bytes where an i386 caller had pushed
sixteen.

⚠ dcomp.idl's interfaces are `local`, so widl emits no C call macros for
them. Refcounting another object's IUnknown goes through its vtable.

⚠ dcomp is a PE module and comes from the host Wine build, not the ps5
one. The change is made there and the rebuilt dcomp.dll copied into the
shipped runtime for both architectures; patching the ps5 tree alone
changes nothing on the console.
diff --git a/dlls/dcomp/device.c b/dlls/dcomp/device.c
index 2744d758e9..0491521ba7 100644
--- a/dlls/dcomp/device.c
+++ b/dlls/dcomp/device.c
@@ -19,19 +19,606 @@
 
 #include "windef.h"
 #include "winbase.h"
+#include "initguid.h"
 #include "objidl.h"
 #include "dxgi.h"
